Singleton Pattern |
------------------+

This pattern makes sure a class has only one single object in the whole program, and everyone can easily use it.


Goals |
------+

1. Only a single instance (object) of the class exists in the program.

2. Provide a way to access this instance from anywhere (globally accessible)


Problem |
--------+

We can't use a regular constructor because new MyClass() creates a brand new object every single time you call it!

We want to get the already existing object instead of making a new one.


Solution |
---------+

1. Make constructor private \(\rightarrow \) Now nobody can use the new keyword from outside!

2. create a public static method, and that method calls private constructor to create the object



When to use it |
---------------+

Use the Singleton pattern when a class in your program should have just a single instance available to all clients; for example, a single database object shared by different parts of the program.


Usages |
-------+

1. Database Connection: Only 1 connection worker exists so your database does not get overwhelmed with too many open links and crash.

2. Logger Service: Only 1 log file worker exists so multiple parts of the app don't lock and crash the text file.

3. Settings Manager: Only 1 settings checker exists so when you change to Dark Mode, the whole app changes together instantly.

4. Cache Manager: Only 1 memory box exists to save data, so the app doesn't have to fetch things from the internet over and over.

5. Thread Pool: Only 1 background task manager exists so your computer doesn't make too many accidental threads and freeze up.



